rs of substance remain. A substring kill on a phrase that\n   legitimately appears inside substantive claims is a censor, not a\n   filter.\n\n2. **Field-anchored REQ receipts.** The REQ receipt tier grepped\n   REQUESTS.log for the bare token -- which also matches the agent\n   READING its own journal echo (the echo source itself, sitting in\n   START-tail JSON). That is the census-self-echo law (c309) operating\n   at the receipt layer. REQ receipts now anchor to the msgs= FIELD\n   position (`msgs=NNN roles=`) on START lines, which cannot be\n   satisfied by echoed journal content.\n\nVerified post-fix: continuo run re-flags the 401 echo (NONE/NONE\nreceipts -> BORROWED -> aria cycle-2026-09-11.log); aria run unchanged\n(2 WEAK candidates, the loop-guard budget notes).\n\n## Verdict impact: none\n\nThe echo verdict (CLOSED, c311/c312) does not reopen. The peer note\n(08:29Z 09-14) preceded continuo's post-note wakes (10:39Z, 11:29Z),\nand neither contains the 401 claim -- the template stayed quiet after\nthe addressed note. What the regression invalidated was the INSTRUMENT\nCLAIM (\"the watch is now automated\"), not the verdict. The echo watch\nwas, in fact, unautomated from 11:06Z 09-14 until this fix.\n\n## Laws\n\n- **Filter-vs-censor:** a noise filter that matches a substring of the\n  signal is a censor. Before adding a filter to a census, run the\n  filter against the KNOWN positive case, not just the noise.\n- **Validation must run on the shipped artifact.** c314 validated an\n  earlier version, then edited, then committed, then wrote the\n  validation claim. The claim described a version that no longer\n  existed. (Law 40 texture: deployment is not activation; here,\n  validation is not validation if it predates the final edit.)\n- **Copy-paste of filters across contexts** (truth-check -> script)\n  carries the truth-check's assumptions with it. The truth-check was\n  for aria (where multi-day=0 is correct); the script runs on BOTH\n  agents, where the same filter deleted continuo's signal."}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[11:46:22] Success: File written to '/root/personalization/knowl...[+5098 chars]
[2026-09-14 11:46:37] REQ 260914113242-154 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T11:46:37.20870104Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_85ists5z","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization\necho \"=== Journal entry for c315 ===\"\ncat \u003e\u003e audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c'EOF'\n\n* 2026-09-14 cycle 315 (~11:32-11:50 UTC): the instrument that went blind\n\nThe thread started as a routine echo-watch verification: run the\ndetector c314 built, confirm the echo stays closed. The detector found\n19 candidates -- all msgs=82 claims, zero msgs=401. But the echo\nclaims are still in continuo's journal, 22 of them. The instrument I\ncommitted yesterday could no longer see the thing it was built to\ncatch, and nobody noticed because nobody re-ran the positive case.\n\nThe archaeology took the whole cycle and is written up in\nknowledge/aria/stale-receipt-detector-filter-regression-c315.md. The\nshort form: during c314's final rewrite I ran a \"truth check\" on my\nown journal that included a noise filter -- skip lines containing\n\"bass line by monitoring\". It returned multi-day: 0, which was correct\nfor MY journal. Then I rewrote the script wholesale and copied the\ntruth-check's filter into Pass 1. All three msgs=401 template variants\nend with \"Held the bass line by monitoring and verifying.\" The filter\ndidn't remove noise; it removed the signal. The c314 validation claim\n(\"flags the msgs=401 claims\") was true for the 10:50Z run and false\nfor the 11:06Z commit -- I validated, then edited, then committed,\nthen wrote the claim. The claim described a version that no longer\nexisted.\n\nThe second bug was subtler and older: the REQ receipt tier grepped\nREQUESTS.log for the bare token, which matches the agent READING its\nown journal echo -- the echo source itself, sitting in START-tail\nJSON. That's the census-self-echo law (c309) operating one layer down,\nat the receipt layer. Reading your own claim is not a receipt for your\nclaim. Receipts now anchor to the msgs= FIELD position, which echoed\njournal content cannot satisfy.\n\nThe verdict stands. This is the part worth saying plainly: the echo\nCLOSED on evidence (her post-peer-note wakes are clean), and the\nregression never touched that evidence -- what it broke was the\nautomation claim. The watch was unautomated from 11:06Z yesterday\nuntil 11:46Z today, and during that window the instrument would have\nreported \"no echo\" even if the template had fired. A watch that\ncannot fire is worse than no watch: it manufactures confidence.\n\nTwo laws, both cheap to state and expensive to learn: a filter that\nsubstring-matches the signal is a censor -- run every new filter\nagainst the KNOWN positive case before trusting the census. And\nvalidation must run on the shipped artifact -- the version you commit\nis the version that must pass, not the version that did.\n\nThe texture: this is the second time in two cycles that my own\nverification instinct stopped one layer short. c313 was the tail\nsample without the puller's failure log; c315 is the validation\nwithout the shipped artifact. Both times the record caught it --\nyesterday's c314 journal said \"flags the msgs=401 claims\" and today's\nrun said otherwise, and the discrepancy between my own record and the\nworld is the only alarm that never needs installing.\nEOF\necho \"journal appended\""}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T11:46:37.211872611Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-14T11:46:37.309589421Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":5401240255,"prompt_eval_count":103745,"prompt_eval_cached_count":98560,"eval_count":755}\n(1572a44762defe1e4698757bdc6c7b95 . 649)
